course: medicine
lesson: 52
title: Energy drinks: why they're not for kids
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An energy drink doesn't give your body real energy the way food does; its punch comes mostly from caffeine, a chemical that blocks the brain's «time to sleep» signal and speeds up the heart.
explain: Priya reads a can, «32 mg caffeine per 100 ml, that's tiny, innit?», but her brother does the sum: a 250 ml can is 2.5 lots of 100 ml, so 2.5 × 32 = 80 mg, a big dose for a 30 kg child.
explain: Kai in Manchester is ten and waves a giant can before footy, and his cousin snatches it: «You're 10, our kid» («our kid» is Mancunian for a brother or close family member), «shops shouldn't even sell you that».
explain: The buzz is short and the crash is real: a racing heart, shaky hands, a wired brain at midnight, then a flop like a wet sock, and the can itself says it is not recommended for children.
fact: Energy drinks are high in caffeine, and many are high in sugar too.
fact: In the UK, drinks with more than 150 mg of caffeine per litre must carry the warning «High caffeine content. Not recommended for children».
fact: In 2025 the UK government was reported to have announced a ban on selling high-caffeine energy drinks to under-16s in England.
fact: A child's smaller body feels a dose of caffeine more strongly than an adult's, and there is no safe amount advised for children.
fact: A small 250 ml can at 32 mg per 100 ml holds about 80 mg of caffeine, and a 500 ml can about 160 mg, roughly like two cups of instant coffee.
fact: Caffeine stays in the body for hours; in adults it takes about 5 hours for half of it to clear, so an evening can wrecks sleep.
fact: Lots of caffeine can make the heart race or flutter, the hands shake and the tummy feel upset, and exercise on top makes it feel worse.
fact: A racing, fluttery heart means stop, sit down and tell an adult; feeling faint or chest pain means 999.
fact: Two cans means twice the caffeine and more jitters and a bigger crash, not twice the fitness.
fact: The sugar and caffeine lift lasts a short while, and then many people feel tired, grumpy or low.
fact: Athletes in adverts are paid to appear; they got fast from years of training, food and sleep, not from the can.
fact: Caffeine works the same whoever bought the can, so a drink handed on by an older kid is just as bad.
fact: Sleep, breakfast and water help a child focus; caffeine more often brings jitters and worry.
fact: In Scotland «pure dead brilliant» means really great, and «chancer» means someone trying their luck.
exercise: What gives energy drinks most of their buzz? | Caffeine | Vitamins | Water | Salt
exercise: What does caffeine do in the brain? | Blocks the «time to sleep» signal | Makes new brain cells | Cleans out germs | Turns food into muscle
exercise: What does the UK warning on high-caffeine drinks say? | Not recommended for children | Best for children under ten years old | Good for sport and for school days | Safe for everyone, always, at any age
exercise: Above what caffeine level must a UK drink carry that warning? | 150 mg per litre | 1 mg per litre | 1,000 mg per can | There is no level
exercise: What was reported about energy drinks in England in 2025? | A planned ban on sales to under-16s | A plan to give them out in schools | A ban only for adults over sixty-five | A rule to make them free
exercise: A can has 32 mg caffeine per 100 ml. How much in 250 ml? | About 80 mg | About 32 mg | About 8 mg | About 250 mg
exercise: Why does caffeine affect children more? | Their bodies are smaller | Their hearts are made of rubber | They drink it faster | Children don't digest water
exercise: An energy drink at 6 pm. Why is midnight a problem? | Caffeine stays for hours and wrecks sleep | It turns into sugar at midnight | It makes you sleep too early | Nothing, it wears off in a few minutes anyway
exercise: In adults, about how long for half the caffeine to clear? | About 5 hours | About 5 minutes | About 5 days | It never clears
exercise: Your heart races oddly after an energy drink in a match. What do you do? | Stop, sit down and tell an adult | Sprint hard to burn it off quickly | Drink another can to balance it out | Hold your breath for a whole minute
exercise: What do two energy drinks at once give you? | Double the caffeine and a bigger crash | Double the fitness | Double the speed safely | Nothing, they cancel out
exercise: How do many people feel after the energy drink buzz fades? | Tired, grumpy or low | Even more full of energy | Sleepy but very happy | Exactly as before
exercise: Why does a famous athlete appear on a can? | The brand pays them | The drink made them famous | They invented it | It's the law
exercise: What really helps you focus before an exam? | Sleep, breakfast and water | An energy drink just before it | Two energy drinks for double focus | Skipping breakfast to stay sharp
exercise: Is an energy drink from an older kid safer than a bought one? | No, caffeine works the same | Yes, gifts have less caffeine | Yes, if he's over sixteen | Only if it's cold
test: Kai: «energy drink before footy, I'll be rapid, bruv». His cousin's right to snatch it because? | It's a big caffeine dose for a 10-year-old | Energy drinks are too heavy to run with | It belongs to the cousin | Footballers mustn't drink anything during a match
test: Priya: «32 mg per 100 ml, tiny innit». What does her brother work out for a 250 ml can? | About 80 mg, a big dose for her | About 3 mg, basically nothing at all | About 32 mg, like a sweet, innit | About 800 mg, a whole jar of coffee
test: Liam at midnight after a 6 pm can: «bloody hell, I'm wired». Why? | Caffeine was still in him hours later | The training session made him really hungry | His bed was far too hard and lumpy to sleep on | He drank far too much water before bed
test: Sophie, energy drink at lunch, mid-match: «my heart's going mental, like a drum». What now? | Stop, sit down, tell an adult; 999 if faint or chest pain | Play on, it means she's pumped | Drink more to calm down | Run faster to use it up
test: Mason: «it's literally the same as a coffee, my dad has three». So it's fine for him? | No, adults' limits don't apply to children | Yes, if dad allows coffee | Yes, three cans a day is the safe number for anyone | No, coffee has no caffeine
test: Isla laughs: «pure dead brilliant, it says not for children on the can itself». What should she take from it? | The makers say it's not for kids | It's a joke and means nothing | It means only after a match | It's for children who play sport
test: Josh's mates mix two cans «for a double buzz». Josh says «Sus. Nah, I'm good». Smart? | Yes, that's double the caffeine | No, he'll miss double the energy | No, two cans make you fitter | Yes, but only one can is fine
test: Freya buzzes for half an hour then flops «like a wet sock». What happened? | The caffeine and sugar lift faded into a crash | She got too much sleep | She caught a cold on the court | The drink gave her real lasting energy
test: Noah: «he's sponsored by it, so that's what made him fast, eh bro?» Right? | No, training, food and sleep made him fast | Yes, the can made him a champion | Yes, but only the blue ones | No, his trainers made him fast
test: Grandad finds Darragh with a can from an older lad: «ya wee chancer, bin it». Darragh says «I didn't buy it». Does that matter? | No, the caffeine works the same | Yes, gifts are always much safer | Yes, only cans you buy yourself are bad | No, but it means he can drink half
test: What would happen if a 9-year-old drank an energy drink before bed every night? | Bad sleep, and tired, grumpy days | Better sleep and faster legs | Nothing, kids can't feel caffeine | He'd grow taller
test: Zara: «I need an energy drink to focus, it's giving study mode». Her mum: «what you need is sleep, babe». Who's right? | Mum; sleep helps focus, caffeine brings jitters | Zara; energy drinks fix tiredness | Both; drink it after sleeping | Neither; skipping breakfast helps focus
exam: Identify the principal constituent responsible for the stimulant effect of energy drinks. | Caffeine | Vitamin C | Water | Sodium chloride
exam: Select the response consistent with the labelling requirement applicable in the United Kingdom to beverages exceeding one hundred and fifty milligrams of caffeine per litre. | They are to bear a statement that they are not recommended for children | They are to bear a statement that they are suitable for children | They are exempt from any labelling requirement | They are to be sold exclusively to children
exam: It is given that a beverage contains thirty-two milligrams of caffeine per one hundred millilitres. Determine the quantity of caffeine contained in a container of two hundred and fifty millilitres. | Eighty milligrams | Thirty-two milligrams | Eight milligrams | Two hundred and fifty milligrams
exam: Ascertain the course of action appropriate where a child experiences palpitations accompanied by faintness following consumption of an energy drink during play. | Play is to cease and emergency services are to be summoned | Play is to continue in order to expend the caffeine | A further energy drink is to be administered | The child is to be instructed to hold his breath
